					<h1>Environmental Systems</h1>
					<p class="subtitle">Time, weather, food, and traversal as living pressure dials</p>
				</header>

				<article class="content">
					<section class="major-section intro-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">IV. Environmental Systems</h2>
						<p>The wild isn't a backdrop — it's an ecosystem that fights, breathes, and remembers.</p>
						<p>Your role as GM is to make the world itself feel alive. Time, weather, hunger, and exhaustion are your invisible monsters — subtle forces that shape every decision.</p>
						<p>The wild is the other hunter. Its storms chase, its nights stalk, its calm lures.</p>
						<p>Every environmental rule is a pacing lever — you're not punishing players; you're reminding them they're small.</p>
					</section>

					<!-- 1. TIME PASSING -->
					<section class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">1. Time Passing &amp; the Day/Night Cycle</h2>
						<blockquote class="quote">"Light fades, tempers shorten, and the wild grows bolder."</blockquote>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>Phases of a Day</h4>
							<div class="table-wrapper">
								<table class="craft-table">
									<thead>
										<tr>
											<th>Time</th>
											<th>Mood &amp; Impact</th>
										</tr>
									</thead>
									<tbody>
										<tr>
											<td>Morning (6 AM–12 PM)</td>
											<td>High visibility; tracks are fresh; monsters sluggish from night hunts.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Afternoon (12 PM–6 PM)</td>
											<td>Heat and fatigue rise; stamina drains faster in arid or humid biomes.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Nightfall (6 PM–12 AM)</td>
											<td>Visibility drops; nocturnal creatures awaken; ambient noise hides danger.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Deep Night (12 AM–6 AM)</td>
											<td>Vision near-zero; apex predators hunt; stamina recovery halved without campfire or torch.</td>
										</tr>
									</tbody>
								</table>
							</div>
						</div>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>Exhaustion from Extended Hunts</h4>
							<ul>
								<li>After 8 hours of active hunting, call for a CON Save (DC 10).</li>
								<li>For every additional 4 hours, repeat; failure adds one Exhaustion level.</li>
								<li>Resting removes exhaustion; forced marches double exhaustion rate.</li>
							</ul>
						</div>

						<div class="callout">
							<strong>When to Use:</strong> Use time progression to pressure indecision. If players dither, the sun sets — monsters migrate, paths fade, and safe rests vanish.
						</div>

						<div class="callout">
							<strong>GM Cues:</strong> Describe the hunt as a slow fade — "The light shifts amber. Your shadows stretch long. The wild changes guards."
						</div>

						<div class="callout">
							<strong>Optional Rule — Dynamic Time Pressure:</strong> Each failed Tracking or Prep check costs 1 hour of daylight. When the party runs out, shift to Nightfall automatically.
						</div>
					</section>

					<!-- 2. CAMPING & RESTING -->
					<section class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">2. Camping &amp; Resting</h2>
						<blockquote class="quote">"The fire is safety, and danger, both at once."</blockquote>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>Camp Types</h4>
							<div class="table-wrapper">
								<table class="craft-table">
									<thead>
										<tr>
											<th>Rest Type</th>
											<th>Duration</th>
											<th>Recovery</th>
											<th>Risk</th>
										</tr>
									</thead>
									<tbody>
										<tr>
											<td>Short Rest (1 hr)</td>
											<td>+Full Stamina, minor healing.</td>
											<td>Reduces 1 exhaustion level.</td>
											<td>Chance of ambush (Instinct check DC 12).</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Long Rest (6 hr)</td>
											<td>+Full HP/Stamina, remove all exhaustion.</td>
											<td>Requires warmth, cover, or fortification.</td>
											<td>If unsafe, roll Instinct (INS) vs monster's Passive Tracking to detect a night threat.</td>
										</tr>
									</tbody>
								</table>
							</div>
						</div>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>Camp Setup (Optional Rolls)</h4>
							<ul>
								<li><strong>Survival (INS)</strong> — choose a good site, avoids exposure.</li>
								<li><strong>Tool Handling (DEX)</strong> — efficient setup, traps/alarms gain advantage.</li>
								<li><strong>Leadership (CHA)</strong> — reduce exhaustion loss, aid recovery narration.</li>
							</ul>
						</div>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>Camp Hazards Table (d6)</h4>
							<div class="table-wrapper">
								<table class="craft-table">
									<thead>
										<tr>
											<th>Roll</th>
											<th>Event</th>
										</tr>
									</thead>
									<tbody>
										<tr><td>1</td><td>Strange cries — nocturnal monster nearby.</td></tr>
										<tr><td>2</td><td>Weather turns: rain, cold, or fog.</td></tr>
										<tr><td>3</td><td>NPC hunter or caravan stumbles upon camp.</td></tr>
										<tr><td>4</td><td>Food spoils or attracts scavengers.</td></tr>
										<tr><td>5</td><td>Equipment mishap: weapon rusts, trap fails.</td></tr>
										<tr><td>6</td><td>Peaceful night</td></tr>
								</tbody>
								</table>
							</div>
							<p class="note">One 1d6 table adds instant replayability to every rest without extra prep.</p>
						</div>

						<div class="callout">
							<strong>GM Prompt:</strong> Describe camp setup as part of play, not a menu. The best camps become safe zones for later hunts.
						</div>

						<div class="callout">
							<strong>When to Use:</strong> When exhaustion looms or morale breaks — camps reset tension, giving pacing structure between phases.
						</div>
					</section>

					<!-- 3. WEATHER -->
					<section class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">3. Weather &amp; Environmental Hazards</h2>
						<blockquote class="quote">"The storm doesn't care who wins the fight."</blockquote>

						<p>Weather isn't cosmetic — it's an active hazard and narrative lever.</p>
						<p>Change weather every few in-game hours, or when crossing sectors.</p>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>Common Weather States</h4>
							<div class="table-wrapper">
								<table class="craft-table">
									<thead>
										<tr>
											<th>Type</th>
											<th>Mechanical Impact</th>
											<th>"When to Use"</th>
										</tr>
									</thead>
									<tbody>
										<tr>
											<td>Clear Skies</td>
											<td>No modifiers.</td>
											<td>Baseline control; early hunts.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Rain</td>
											<td>+2 DC to climb; ranged attacks at disadvantage. Tracks fade after 10 min.</td>
											<td>Add tension mid-hunt or soften a fire-heavy fight.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Fog</td>
											<td>–2 INS for detection; no advantage from elevation.</td>
											<td>Use to reset positioning or hide monsters.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Storm</td>
											<td>Random lightning (DC 14 DEX save); ranged attacks at disadvantage.</td>
											<td>Mid-to-high-tier chaos fights.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Extreme Cold</td>
											<td>1 CON save (DC 12) per hour or gain 1 exhaustion.</td>
											<td>In tundra or caves; push attrition.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Heatwave</td>
											<td>+1 STA cost per Action; water loss (see Food &amp; Water).</td>
											<td>Desert or open plains; saps endurance.</td>
										</tr>
									</tbody>
								</table>
							</div>
						</div>

						<div class="callout">
							<strong>GM Cue:</strong> Always describe the feel of the weather before the penalty. Let players sense the change coming.
							<br />"Thunder rolls across the ridge — the air tastes of iron."
						</div>

						<p>Each biome has a dominant weather pattern. When you roll for weather, favor that pattern 2/3 of the time and use others as anomalies — a freak storm, heatwave, or spore fog.</p>

						<div class="callout">
							<strong>Example:</strong> Forest → Rain/Fog; Desert → Heatwave/Clear; Tundra → Cold/Storm.
						</div>
					</section>

					<!-- 4. FOOD, WATER & SUSTENANCE -->
					<section class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">4. Food, Water &amp; Sustenance</h2>
						<blockquote class="quote">"The wild feeds itself first."</blockquote>
						<blockquote class="quote">"Every meal in the wild is a promise to survive tomorrow."</blockquote>

						<p>Eating isn't just bookkeeping. It's the heartbeat before the hunt — the quiet ritual where hunters prepare, bond, and choose how they want to face the world.</p>
						<p>As the GM, meals are your way to:</p>
						<ul>
							<li>Signal tone ("A cold ration on a cliff = tension; a feast by fire = respite"),</li>
							<li>Encourage prep play, and</li>
							<li>Reward planning without adding more gear math.</li>
						</ul>

						<!-- Foraging -->
						<div class="subsection">
							<h3>🪶 Foraging &amp; Gathering (Downtime or Interlude)</h3>
							<p>The wilderness always provides… if you're willing to risk poison, predators, or time.</p>

							<div class="rule-section">
								<h4>Checks</h4>
								<ul>
									<li><strong>Survival (INS)</strong> → track, gather, and hunt.</li>
									<li><strong>Resource ID (INT)</strong> → identify herbs, fruits, or reagents.</li>
									<li><strong>DC 10:</strong> Common foodstuffs — nuts, small game, wild greens.</li>
									<li><strong>DC 14:</strong> Rare herbs, healing roots, unusual spices.</li>
									<li><strong>DC 16+:</strong> Monster-linked reagents or alchemical flora.</li>
								</ul>
							</div>

							<div class="rule-section">
								<h4>Results</h4>
								<div class="table-wrapper">
									<table class="craft-table">
										<thead>
											<tr>
												<th>Result</th>
												<th>Outcome</th>
											</tr>
										</thead>
										<tbody>
											<tr>
												<td>Success</td>
												<td>Gain 1–2 ingredients (GM choice by biome).</td>
											</tr>
											<tr>
												<td>Critical Success (Nat 20)</td>
												<td>Gain 1 rare ingredient and a crafting material.</td>
											</tr>
											<tr>
												<td>Failure</td>
												<td>Find spoiled or tainted food → CON Save DC 12 or become Poisoned.</td>
											</tr>
											<tr>
												<td>Critical Failure</td>
												<td>Poisoned and nearby monster is drawn to scent of rot or blood.</td>
											</tr>
										</tbody>
									</table>
								</div>
							</div>

							<div class="callout">
								<strong>GM Use:</strong> Foraging scenes create pacing between hunts. They're great "soft encounters" — low danger, high character expression.
							</div>
						</div>

						<!-- Cooking & Meal Buffs -->
						<div class="subsection">
							<h3>🔥 Cooking &amp; Meal Buffs</h3>
							<p>Meals grant short-term bonuses that reinforce how the party prepared.</p>
							<p>Every dish tells a story: spice for nerve, fat for stamina, herbs for resilience.</p>

							<div class="rule-section">
								<h4>🍛 Cooking Procedure</h4>
								<ul>
									<li><strong>Set the Scene:</strong> A meal always takes at least 1 hour and a heat source (campfire, stove, volcanic vent).</li>
									<li><strong>Choose a Cook:</strong> One hunter leads the preparation (usually uses Tool Handling (DEX) or Resource ID (INT)).</li>
									<li><strong>Spend Ingredients:</strong> At least one base (meat, grain, mushroom) and one flavor (herb, spice, oil).</li>
									<li><strong>Roll &amp; Serve:</strong> Cook rolls vs DC 12 (routine) or DC 14–16 (complex).</li>
									<li><strong>Success:</strong> The meal grants a buff for the next hunt.</li>
									<li><strong>Critical Success:</strong> Buff lasts two hunts or doubles its effect.</li>
									<li><strong>Failure:</strong> No buff; flavor goes wrong.</li>
									<li><strong>Critical Failure:</strong> Food poisoning (CON save DC 12 or 1 level Exhaustion).</li>
								</ul>
							</div>

							<div class="rule-section">
								<h4>🍴 Meal Buff Table</h4>
								<div class="table-wrapper">
									<table class="craft-table">
										<thead>
											<tr>
												<th>Dish Type</th>
												<th>Description</th>
												<th>Effect</th>
											</tr>
										</thead>
										<tbody>
											<tr>
												<td>Warm Meal</td>
												<td>Balanced comfort food — roasted game, root stew.</td>
												<td>+1 Stamina regeneration per turn for 1 combat.</td>
											</tr>
											<tr>
												<td>Hearty Stew</td>
												<td>Dense broth, heavy grains.</td>
												<td>+2 HP per hour (non-combat recovery) until next rest.</td>
											</tr>
											<tr>
												<td>Spiced Ration</td>
												<td>Portable dried food.</td>
												<td>+1 to next 3 CON saves.</td>
											</tr>
											<tr>
												<td>Hunter's Feast</td>
												<td>Luxurious or rare ingredients.</td>
												<td>+1 to Attack &amp; Damage rolls for first combat of next hunt.</td>
											</tr>
											<tr>
												<td>Calming Tea</td>
												<td>Herbal infusion, aromatic.</td>
												<td>Remove 1 mental condition (Dazed, Taunted, Frightened).</td>
											</tr>
											<tr>
												<td>Sharpening Broth</td>
												<td>Salty, mineral-rich brew.</td>
												<td>Next 3 Called Shots ignore +1 AR.</td>
											</tr>
											<tr>
												<td>Storm Ration</td>
												<td>High-energy nuts, sugars, or salts.</td>
												<td>+2 temporary Stamina at start of next hunt.</td>
											</tr>
											<tr>
												<td>Nightwatch Brew</td>
												<td>Bitter drink brewed under moonlight.</td>
												<td>Advantage on INS checks vs ambush for one night cycle.</td>
											</tr>
										</tbody>
									</table>
								</div>
								<p class="note">(GM: Use 1–2 buffs per meal max. Keep it flavorful, not crunchy.)</p>
							</div>

							<p>Certain monster parts double as "culinary reagents." Using them in a dish adds an elemental trait (e.g., Thunder Gland → meal grants resistance to Shock or bonus to Lightning damage).</p>

							<div class="rule-section highlight">
								<h4>Mid-Hunt Meal Synergy</h4>
								<p>The hunters have been tracking a Sporeback Basilisk through a swamp for hours. Exhausted, they stop on a mossy ridge to cook a meal. They have two basilisk fillets (base) and a pouch of tangy swamp herbs (flavor). The cook uses Tool Handling (DEX) to prepare a Spiced Ration and rolls a 17 vs DC 14 (success). The party chooses Sharpening Broth as the effect, gaining +1 AR ignore on their next 3 Called Shots. Because they cooked with basilisk meat, the GM adds an elemental twist: the broth's spores grant resistance to poison for the next hunt. The hunters mark the buff and return to the wetlands with renewed focus.</p>
							</div>

							<div class="rule-section">
								<h4>🍳 Cooking Traits &amp; Mishaps (Optional Flavor)</h4>
								<p>Add personality to cooks — or turn mishaps into story.</p>
								<div class="table-wrapper">
									<table class="craft-table">
										<thead>
											<tr>
												<th>Outcome</th>
												<th>Example</th>
											</tr>
										</thead>
										<tbody>
											<tr>
												<td>Perfect Timing</td>
												<td>Everyone is energized exhaustion recovery +1 on next rest.</td>
											</tr>
											<tr>
												<td>Burnt Meal</td>
												<td>No buff; campfire draws nearby predators (roll for encounter).</td>
											</tr>
											<tr>
												<td>Experimental Brew</td>
												<td>Buff unknown until triggered. Roll a random benefit (or backfire) from the table above.</td>
											</tr>
											<tr>
												<td>Monster Meat Meal</td>
												<td>Grants unique buff tied to that creature's element (e.g., Fire Beast Steak → resist Burned once).</td>
											</tr>
										</tbody>
									</table>
								</div>
							</div>
						</div>

						<!-- Water -->
						<div class="subsection">
							<h3>🥤 Water &amp; Hydration</h3>
							<p>Hydration is only tracked during long hunts or arid biomes.</p>

							<div class="table-wrapper">
								<table class="craft-table">
									<thead>
										<tr>
											<th>Situation</th>
											<th>Check</th>
											<th>Consequence</th>
										</tr>
									</thead>
									<tbody>
										<tr>
											<td>No water for 1 day</td>
											<td>CON save DC 10</td>
											<td>Gain 1 Exhaustion.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>No water for 2+ days</td>
											<td>CON save DC 12</td>
											<td>Exhaustion + disadvantage on STR/DEX checks.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Contaminated source</td>
											<td>CON save DC 12</td>
											<td>Poisoned for 1 hour.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Purified water</td>
											<td>Requires Fire Kit or Herbalism proficiency</td>
											<td>Safe</td>
										</tr>
									</tbody>
								</table>
							</div>

							<div class="callout">
								<strong>GM Use:</strong> Make water scarcity a pressure dial, not a punishment. If a biome's harshness needs emphasis, tie thirst to decision points — "Push through to the canyon or camp by the dry riverbed?"
							</div>
						</div>

						<!-- GM Role in Food Scenes -->
						<div class="subsection">
							<h3>🎭 GM Role in Food Scenes</h3>
							<p>Meals are micro-stories. Use them to:</p>
							<ul>
								<li><strong>Show passage of time.</strong> ("The stew simmers as dusk swallows the ridge.")</li>
								<li><strong>Reveal personalities.</strong> Cooks, picky eaters, superstitious rituals — all develop tone.</li>
								<li><strong>Telegraph danger.</strong> "The meat hisses oddly — the herbs spark green." (Cue: contamination or monster residue.)</li>
								<li><strong>Reward flavor play.</strong> Creative meal descriptions deserve mechanical payoff. The more sensory it sounds, the tastier the bonus.</li>
							</ul>
						</div>

						<!-- Meal Synergy -->
						<div class="subsection">
							<h3>🧠 Optional System — "Meal Synergy" (Playtest Module)</h3>
							<p>If multiple hunters eat different dishes, their effects can synergize for cinematic boosts.</p>
							<p>Example combos:</p>
							<ul>
								<li><strong>Hearty Stew + Sharpening Broth</strong> → First critical hit this hunt restores +2 Stamina.</li>
								<li><strong>Warm Meal + Calming Tea</strong> → Advantage on first fear save.</li>
								<li><strong>Hunter's Feast + Nightwatch Brew</strong> → +2 to Initiative for first encounter.</li>
							</ul>
							<p>You can limit synergy to one per session for balance — but it gives cooks and planners a new minigame: "What should we eat before the hunt?"</p>
						</div>
					</section>

					<!-- 5. TRAVERSAL -->
					<section class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">5. Traversal &amp; Survival Tools</h2>
						<blockquote class="quote">"Every step is a gamble."</blockquote>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>Terrain Effects</h4>
							<div class="table-wrapper">
								<table class="craft-table">
									<thead>
										<tr>
											<th>Terrain</th>
											<th>Movement Impact</th>
											<th>Typical Checks</th>
										</tr>
									</thead>
									<tbody>
										<tr>
											<td>Flat/Open</td>
											<td>Normal pace.</td>
											<td>None.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Dense Forest/Jungle</td>
											<td>–10 ft movement; stealth advantage.</td>
											<td>Tracking (INS), Stealth (DEX).</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Cliffs/Mountains</td>
											<td>Climb halves speed; fall risk.</td>
											<td>Athletics (STR), Acrobatics (DEX).</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Swamps/Mudflats</td>
											<td>Speed halved; exhaustion builds.</td>
											<td>Endurance (CON).</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Deep Water</td>
											<td>Swim speed replaces walk; tool penalties.</td>
											<td>CON save every 3 rounds if unaided.</td>
										</tr>
									</tbody>
								</table>
							</div>
						</div>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>Essential Tools</h4>
							<ul>
								<li><strong>Ropes &amp; Hooks:</strong> +2 to climbing checks.</li>
								<li><strong>Fire Kit:</strong> Required for warmth; attracts monsters (Roll Instinct DC 12 to detect).</li>
								<li><strong>Glider:</strong> Negates fall damage once per rest.</li>
								<li><strong>Scent Mask:</strong> Monsters roll disadvantage to detect you.</li>
								<li><strong>Raft/Boat:</strong> Reduces fatigue in aquatic travel.</li>
							</ul>
						</div>

						<div class="callout">
							<strong>When to Use:</strong> Introduce traversal challenges between major hunts or to reshape terrain mid-combat. Keep rolls rare but meaningful — one good check should open a route.
						</div>

						<p>Terrain tools don't just help in travel — they shape the battlefield. A rope rigged before combat or a glider used mid-fall can turn a failed save into a cinematic recovery.</p>
					</section>

					<!-- 6. NIGHT HUNTS -->
					<section class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">6. Night Hunts &amp; Nocturnal Threats</h2>
						<blockquote class="quote">"The dark doesn't hide you — it blinds you."</blockquote>

						<p>Night alters risk and reward. It's where rare materials and predators thrive. Light reveals safety but removes stealth. Torches grant full vision but impose disadvantage on Stealth and advantage on enemy detection.</p>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>Night Effects</h4>
							<ul>
								<li>Vision reduced to 30 ft without light source.</li>
								<li>Monsters gain advantage on detection.</li>
								<li>Loot quality +1 tier for nocturnal kills.</li>
								<li>Certain monsters only spawn after dusk.</li>
							</ul>
						</div>

						<div class="callout">
							<strong>Optional GM Rule –</strong> Darkness Pressure
							<br />Each hour after dusk, raise encounter DC by +1 as fatigue and fear mount.
						</div>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>Nocturnal Examples</h4>
							<div class="table-wrapper">
								<table class="craft-table">
									<thead>
										<tr>
											<th>Creature</th>
											<th>Behavior</th>
											<th>Night Impact</th>
										</tr>
									</thead>
									<tbody>
										<tr>
											<td>Varnis (Shadow Lynx)</td>
											<td>Ambush predator; teleports between shadows.</td>
											<td>Stealth advantage; retreats at dawn.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Noctilura (Moon Serpent)</td>
											<td>Hypnotic bioluminescence.</td>
											<td>Stuns on visual fails; weak to sudden light.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Gorhull (Mud Boar)</td>
											<td>Becomes enraged at night.</td>
											<td>Double stamina recovery; drops rare tusk.</td>
										</tr>
									</tbody>
								</table>
							</div>
						</div>

						<div class="callout">
							<strong>When to Use:</strong> Use night hunts as deliberate tension arcs—harder, riskier, more rewarding. Reward preparation (torches, flares, scent masks) with meaningful survival chances.
						</div>
					</section>

					<!-- 7. FAILED HUNTS -->
					<section class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">7. Failed Hunts &amp; Recovery</h2>
						<blockquote class="quote">"Failure isn't death — it's migration."</blockquote>

						<p>Failure should move the story forward, not reset it.</p>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>If the Hunters Retreat or Lose:</h4>
							<ul>
								<li>The monster migrates sectors or heals (50–100% HP depending on downtime).</li>
								<li>New environmental hazards appear — wounded beasts cause regional agitation.</li>
								<li>Hunters lose temporary materials but retain progression.</li>
								<li>The next hunt begins with a new tracking DC (+2 harder) — the world remembers.</li>
							</ul>
						</div>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>Total Knockout Scenarios (Optional)</h4>
							<div class="table-wrapper">
								<table class="craft-table">
									<thead>
										<tr>
											<th>Outcome</th>
											<th>GM Use</th>
										</tr>
									</thead>
									<tbody>
										<tr>
											<td>Capture</td>
											<td>Transition into escape or rescue phase.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Rescue Mission</td>
											<td>Secondary NPCs or rival hunters intervene.</td>
										</tr>
										<tr>
											<td>Long-Term Injury</td>
											<td>Reduce CON or DEX temporarily; heal through downtime.</td>
										</tr>
									</tbody>
								</table>
							</div>
						</div>

						<div class="callout">
							<strong>When to Use:</strong> Only when stakes deserve persistence. Failure should shape the ecology, not erase it.
						</div>

						<p>When a monster migrates, roll a new Sector Event immediately — the world shifts around its absence.</p>
					</section>

					<!-- 8. GM ENVIRONMENTAL PRINCIPLES -->
					<section class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">GM Environmental Principles</h2>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>The world moves without them.</h4>
							<p>Don't pause nature because the hunters camped. Weather, migrations, and rival hunts progress off-screen.</p>
						</div>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>Use environment to pace, not punish.</h4>
							<p>When players stall, shift the conditions — heatwave, fog, or storm. Each change pushes new tactics.</p>
						</div>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>Reward adaptation.</h4>
							<p>The wild isn't unfair — it's responsive. Hunters who respect it should feel clever, not lucky.</p>
						</div>

						<div class="rule-section">
							<h4>Link cause and consequence.</h4>
							<p>A burned forest changes wind, visibility, and creature behavior later. Track what players alter.</p>
						</div>

						<blockquote class="quote final-quote">The wild isn't hostile — it's consistent. Hunters fail when they forget its rhythm, not its cruelty.</blockquote>
					</section>
				</article>
			</div>
		</main>

		<style>
